s Sovereign wealth fund and the  global economy well watch out because in  2023 alone the Norwegian government  expects to earn more than 130 million  dollars from the exploitation of crude  oil and natural gas and that's not all  at the moment revenues for 2022 are  estimated at almost 120 billion dollars  and do you know how much they earned in  2021 a little less than 30 billion in  other words thanks to the war in Ukraine  Norway will receive in 2022 and 2023 the  equivalent income of eight regular years  and you know what a large part of this  income will be earmarked for its  Sovereign wealth fund and this is no  joke Norway already owns 1.3 of all  shares in the world companies such as  Nestle apple and Microsoft have the  Norwegian Sovereign wealth fund as one  of their largest shareholders and stay  tuned because here comes the really  interesting part one advantage of being  a shareholder of a company is that if  you have enough shares you can influence  its direction well until a few years ago  this Sovereign wealth fund did not  pursue any influence over the companies  in which it invested let's say that it  limited itself to excluding certain  types of companies from its portfolio  basically arms manufacturers producers  of hydrocarbons tobacco coal or  companies with dubious links to Human  Rights something that of course it  continues to do  [Music]  for example the fund recently put  Canada's Bombardier and South Korea's  Hyundai glovis on a watch list for  possible sale from its investments in  the case of Hyundai it was because of  its alleged use of scrap yards in  Pakistan and Bangladesh where labor  conditions border on human right  violations at least according to the  fund in the case of Bombardier the  reason was some suspicions of corruption  in various countries then this same fund  has also had controversies with the  Chinese government they divested all  shares in the sports company Lee ning  due to an apparent violation of human  rights 